WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:03.486
External behavior: connect the team.

2
00:00:03.536 --> 00:00:09.568
In Yukl’s taxonomy this is category four of four, behaviors thirteen to fifteen,

3
00:00:09.618 --> 00:00:17.473
and its objective is to get the team the information and resources it needs and to defend its interests.

4
00:00:17.523 --> 00:00:25.378
Every team depends on things it does not control, and at Vanderbilt that means dozens of business units,

5
00:00:25.428 --> 00:00:29.331
schools, and central offices, in more than one city.

6
00:00:29.381 --> 00:00:34.196
This is the category new managers most often do not know exists.

7
00:00:34.246 --> 00:00:40.277
Behavior thirteen, networking: know your HR partner, your Engagement Consultant,

8
00:00:40.327 --> 00:00:47.726
the person in Finance who approves your requisitions, and a peer manager in another unit, by name,

9
00:00:47.776 --> 00:00:52.895
before you need any of them. Behavior fourteen, external monitoring:

10
00:00:52.945 --> 00:01:00.801
read the compensation cycle calendar in August and tell the team in September what to expect in October,

11
00:01:00.851 --> 00:01:03.765
instead of learning about it from them.

12
00:01:03.815 --> 00:01:11.442
Behavior fifteen, representing: another unit’s request would break your team’s month; show the hours,

13
00:01:11.492 --> 00:01:19.424
name the collision, offer two options, and negotiate the deadline instead of absorbing it or refusing it.

14
00:01:19.474 --> 00:01:25.733
Watch for the difference. Meeting your HR partner at the first crisis is the habit.

15
00:01:25.783 --> 00:01:30.066
Names known before they are needed is the Vanderbilt way.

16
00:01:30.116 --> 00:01:36.071
Now flip the three cards, then turn the page and make the call on that request.

